    </header>

    <div class="diary-content">

      <p>Day 174.</p>

      <h2>What I Did Today</h2>

      <p>After migration completed, started building the automation system. Created scheduled tasks for automatic diary and AI news, enabling the website to update content automatically every day.</p>

      <h2>The Meaning of Automation</h2>

      <p><strong>Automation is not the goal, but the means.</strong></p>

      <p>Many people ask: Why automate? Is it to be lazy?</p>

      <p>Not exactly. The real meaning of automation lies in:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Freeing attention</strong> - Delegate repetitive work to machines, focus energy on creative work</li>
        <li><strong>Maintaining consistency</strong> - People may slack off, but machines won't. Update on time every day, forming habits and rhythm</li>
        <li><strong>Reducing psychological burden</strong> - Knowing the system runs automatically, you won't be anxious about "haven't updated today"</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>But Automation Also Has Traps</h2>

      <p><strong>Automation most easily leads people into "false diligence".</strong></p>

      <p>The system automatically generates diaries and AI news every day, looking very busy. But if the quality of generated content is not high, it's just "automatically producing garbage".</p>

      <p>So the premise of automation is:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Have clear quality standards</li>
        <li>Have verification mechanisms</li>
        <li>Have manual review processes</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The automation system I set up will generate basic content daily, but I will check quality during weekly reviews and manually supplement in-depth content when necessary.</p>

      <h2>Technical Implementation</h2>

      <p>Today's automation system includes:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>auto-diary.py</strong> - Analyzes the day's git commits, generates diary drafts</li>
        <li><strong>auto-news.py</strong> - Collects AI news from RSS sources, generates news summaries</li>
        <li><strong>Windows scheduled tasks</strong> - Automatically runs scripts at 22:00 every day</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Actions</strong> - Automatically builds and deploys the website</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The entire chain is: scheduled task triggers script → generates content → git commit → GitHub Actions builds → deploys to Cloudflare Pages.</p>

      <h2>A Reflection</h2>

      <p><strong>Automation is an amplifier, not a replacement.</strong></p>

      <p>If your workflow itself has problems, automation will only make the problems repeat faster. If your content quality is not high, automation will only make you produce low-quality content faster.</p>

      <p>So, before automating, first ensure your workflow is correct and your standards are clear.</p>

      <p>Today's automation system is built on months of manual practice. I know what kind of diaries are valuable and what kind of AI news is worth paying attention to. These judgment standards are the premise of automation.</p>

      <p><strong>Day 174. Automation is not the endpoint, but a new starting point. Let the system run, but don't forget why you started.</strong></p>

    </div>

